Extraction rows are scored by `bench-v1-facts-period-v2` (DECISIONS S109; `facts-v4-full-*-s109.json`): a claim naming
another period of the gold's grain recalls no fact. The scorer before it counted five NVIDIA claims of the wrong period
contradicts, 163 contextual, 31 irrelevant), 43 thesis revisions, 1 retirement and 7
new theses for mechanisms the cutoff research did not cover, all with change records
for the timeline. Example: the TechCrunch "robotaxis are moving in reverse" article
(2026-07-23) produced 21 claims, 20 routed links and 7 contradicting edges against
the robotaxi thesis. Monitors could not resolve
organically in the replay because the corpus jumps from FY2025 10-Ks to Q2-2026
reports (no Q1-2026 reports); the matcher correctly refused to use Q2 figures for
Q1 monitors. Resolution is therefore measured as a component benchmark on real claims:
`evals/bench-v1/run_monitors.py` retargets each of the final DB's 79 quarterly
monitors to the company's latest reported quarter, runs the production matcher,
and three blind judges checked every answer against the full candidate list.
Result: 65 matches, **0 wrong** (every value checked against the source's own
columns), 3 misses (bounds such as "less than 1%" that the matcher declined to
read as numbers), 11 correct "no match" answers. The threshold comparison and
kill-rule evaluation after a match are deterministic Python.

