   239	**3. Deduplication needs a permissive search representation and a strict identity decision.**
   240	
   241	Use two explicitly versioned representations:
   242	
   243	```text
   244	Recall text
   245	  object name and identifier
   246	  question
   247	  focus
   248	  measure and scope
   249	  explicit period start/end
   250	  basis, unit and event type
   251	
   252	Comparison record
   253	  all of the above
   254	  key version and scope provenance
   255	  ladder and due date
   256	  relevant initiating quotation and source identity
   257	```
   258	
   259	Run a text-only arm and a key-enriched arm. Adding labels to an embedding does not make the model enforce them. Compare their measured retrieval quality rather than assuming the richer string wins.
   260	
   261	Keep these decisions in code where the current contract already determines them:
   262	
   263	| Comparison | Decision |
   264	|---|---|
   265	| Different explicit accounting bases | Not identical |
   266	| Level versus growth; USD versus percent | Not identical |
   267	| Different periods | Not a merge; test succession separately |
   268	| Launch versus shipment versus deployment | Not identical |
   269	| Different code-owned scopes | Not identical |
   270	| Complete, trusted version-2 automatic keys agree | Preserve the current deterministic identity rule |
   271	| Free scope wording differs, or a field is ambiguous | Retrieve for comparison; do not infer equality or inequality from spelling alone |
   272	
   273	This is the practical meaning of D154’s “necessary, not sufficient” boundary. Literal equality of free-text scopes is not a prerequisite for finding a semantic duplicate. Conversely, compatible fields do not establish that two loosely defined questions are identical.
   274	
   275	The semantic comparator should distinguish:
   276	
   277	`same quantity/event`, `narrower or broader`, `related`, `different`, `insufficient information`.
   278	
   279	Those labels feed the existing merge/new/succession rules. A general relevance score cannot stand in for them. In particular, treating “part of” as “same” recreates the Amazon-capex and segment-revenue mistakes.
   280	
   281	The initiating quotation matters in S.v16/S.v48: the thesis wording alone suggests broad versus narrow revenue, while the evidence explains that both refer to standalone CPU sales. This can be tested by adding already stored source material to the comparison record; it requires no extraction-contract change.
   282	
   283	For evidence, represent **who asserted what, about which event or quantity, for which period, and when**. Preserve the original source separately from the publisher retelling it. A repeated estimate, a revised estimate and an official denial may be very close embeddings but require three different actions.
   284	
   285	A cross-encoder can rank these pairs. It cannot be assumed to classify them reliably without labels. Its sigmoid score is not a calibrated probability of identity.
