     4	
     5	## 1. What the two steps do in this system
     6	
     7	| Step | Question it answers | Today | In the POC |
     8	|---|---|---|---|
     9	| Recall | which stored theses (or groups) could this candidate be the same as, or belong with? | trigram search over object + focus + question, top 10, plus the item's own objects' theses; groups by object and a lexical search | an embedding over a versioned "recall text" (object, question, focus, measure, scope, period, basis, unit, event type), top 20, unioned with the key candidates and the lexical candidates |
    10	| Rerank | for the candidate against each recalled thesis or group: same quantity, part of, same matter, different? | the matching call (one generative call per item, deciding candidates, dependencies and evidence at once) and the extraction call's group citation | a cross-encoder scores (candidate, thesis) and (candidate, group card) pairs; a decision layer turns scores and the code-owned key into `same / part-of / same-matter / different / abstain` |
    11	
    12	Two facts from the stored data decide the shape (Codex's census, verified against the ledgers): recall is not the failure today — in every stored duplicate the right earlier thesis was already in the list the model saw (S record 172, C records 78/88/96, G 43, E 44); the failure is the identity judgement. And the week's one grouping failure class (customer deployments split from the rollout) disappeared once the group was shown with its names (poc-v21: SK and NAVER both cited the Rubin group). So the reranker's job here is narrower than "find the right one": it is to decide membership and identity consistently and cheaply, and to catch what the model misses (Vera CPU → Rubin; Meta's and Microsoft's capex → the capex cycle).
    13	
    14	## 2. The gold
    15	
    16	`poc/eval/gold/grouping-identity-gold-2026-10-25.json`, digest in `SHA256SUMS`. Labelled as an analyst from the stored questions and their initiating evidence: 3 `same` sets (one provisional), 11 `related_not_same` pairs (the hard negatives: GAAP vs non-GAAP, level vs growth, 2026 vs 2027, population differences, part-of), 4 `different` pairs, and four matters with required and forbidden members across the English and Chinese ledgers (Rubin rollout; the hyperscaler capex cycle including each company's capex; the OpenAI–NVIDIA financing, scorable only on the Chinese ledger; China chip export controls), plus the separate company matters and four uncertain cases. The analyst's call that departs from the stored data: the capex cycle group should hold every hyperscaler's own capex thesis, so the grouped week's Amazon-in / Meta-out inconsistency (F7) is scored as two misses, not one error.
    17	
    18	## 3. Reranker options, grounded
    19	
    20	| Option | Facts | Fit |
    21	|---|---|---|
    22	| `BAAI/bge-reranker-v2-m3` | 0.6B parameters on the bge-m3 base, multilingual, query–passage pairs → relevance score (sigmoid to [0,1]), 512-token input, Apache-2.0, meant to rerank the top 100 of a retriever ([model card](https://huggingface.co/BAAI/bge-reranker-v2-m3)) | the default local cross-encoder: free to run, both languages, fits a thesis card (≈150 tokens) and a group card (≈300 tokens) within 512 |
    23	| `jinaai/jina-reranker-v2-base-multilingual` | 0.3B, 1,024-token context with sliding window, BEIR 53.17 / MKQA 54.83 nDCG@10, CC-BY-NC-4.0: research only, commercial use through Jina's API ([model card](https://huggingface.co/jinaai/jina-reranker-v2-base-multilingual)) | a second local arm for the POC only; not for the product without a licence |
    24	| hosted rerankers (Cohere Rerank) | the pricing page shows dedicated-instance prices ($4.00/hour or $2,500/month for a Medium instance) and defines a search unit as one query with up to 100 documents, with no per-search price listed ([pricing](https://cohere.com/pricing)) | not for a $0.05 POC |
    25	| LLM listwise reranking (RankGPT) | properly instructed LLMs rank passages competitively with, and sometimes better than, supervised cross-encoders such as monoT5 on TREC and BEIR; a distilled 440M model beat a 3B supervised model on BEIR ([Sun et al., EMNLP 2023](https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.09542)) | this is what the matching call already is, with more structure; at the published gpt-6-luna rates a listwise decision of ~1,500 input / 200 output tokens costs about $0.00025, so cost is not the reason to leave it |
    26	
    27	What the literature does not give us: a cross-encoder trained on passage relevance outputs *relevance*, not *identity*. "Hyperscaler capex 2026" and "hyperscaler capex 2027" are maximally relevant to each other and must never merge. A relevance reranker therefore cannot be the identity judge on its own; it can rank, and the key decides what a high rank may mean.
    28	
    29	## 4. Implementation on this repository's data (offline, nothing written to any ledger)
    30	
    31	1. **Snapshot and reconstruct** (Codex's stage A): for every stored matching decision (G 145, E 210, C 155, S 315 records), rebuild the library as it stood at that sequence number (open theses, keys, groups with members), the candidate as the handover carried it, and the destination actually written (`processing_steps` step `written`, since `match_records.written_json` is null everywhere).
    32	2. **Recall text and cards**: thesis card = object name, question, focus, key fields written out; group card = name, scope, aliases, up to five member focuses. Version both strings; store the hash.
    33	3. **Recall arms** (exact scans, no index server at this size): current lists; exact-key candidates; dense top-20 (`text-embedding-3-small`, the index the idea route already uses; ≈ 50k tokens for all four ledgers, about $0.001; local `bge-m3` as the free twin); union with a fixed fusion. Metric: recall@k against the gold `same` targets and, for groups, against required members' groups.
    34	4. **Rerank arms**: (a) `bge-reranker-v2-m3` on (candidate card, thesis card) and (candidate card, group card); (b) the same with the candidate's initiating quote appended to its card (source-assisted; S v16/v48 is only decidable from the quote); (c) gpt-6-luna listwise over the top 10 with the same labels, 3 fresh samples, as the control that is also the current method. Local arms cost $0; (c) ≈ 24 inputs × 3 samples × 2 arms ≈ 144 calls ≈ $0.04 at the published rates.
    35	5. **Decision layer** (code, measured on the dev half of the gold, scored once on the rest): key gate first (different basis, unit, period, event type, or code-owned scope → not the same; same complete code-owned key → the same, as today); then the reranker score with two thresholds: above the high one → `same` (identity) only if the key allows; between → `same-matter` (membership); below → `different`; an `abstain` band where the generative call is still asked. For groups: a thesis joins every group whose card scores above the membership threshold, zero or several, never exactly one.
    36	6. **Report** by the six layers of the grouping-acceptance contract: content coverage (a reranker cannot create the missing OpenAI thesis), membership (required recovered / forbidden produced), identity (false merges on the hard negatives, duplicates recovered), evidence fidelity (not in this POC), sequential behaviour (no future thesis in any list), stability and cost (three samples, per-decision cost at the published rates).
    37	
    38	Budget: $0 for steps 1–2 and the local arms; ≤ $0.05 embeddings; ≤ $0.10 for the generative control at the published rates. One evening if the gold holds; stop with the baseline if the labels need work.
    39	
    40	## 5. The outcome to expect, said before running
    41	
    42	- **Recall**: already saturated on a library of 45–94 theses; dense recall will match or slightly beat the trigram lists (the idea route's cached vectors already put the gold thesis in the top 10 for 17–18 of 18 ideas, Codex §2). No visible product change from recall alone.
    43	- **Grouping**: this is where a reranker should pay, but only with the source in hand. Checked on the stored g1 card (name, scope, aliases `Blackwell`, `Rubin`, `AI factories`, `AI infrastructure`) against the thesis texts: Meta's and Microsoft's capex theses share `capital`, `infrastructure`, `investment` with the card, so a text-only cross-encoder should recover them (the F7 misses) and should reject RTX Spark and NemoClaw, which share nothing; the hard negative is NVIDIA's consolidated revenue (shares `infrastructure`, `revenue`), which the threshold must keep out. The Vera CPU thesis shares only `revenue` with the card and the NAVER factory shares nothing: both are joinable only through the source text (the press release says the factory runs Vera Rubin and Blackwell; the transcript says Vera is the Rubin platform's CPU), which is how the model joined NAVER in poc-v21 (it read the source while citing the group). So arm (b), the reranker given the candidate's initiating quote, is the one that can recover Vera CPU and NAVER; arm (a) cannot, and a hit from it would mean the cross-encoder answered from remembered product knowledge, which does not count. Expect it to fail on F1 (it will rank the capex aggregates and the Rubin shipments both highly against the fused g1, because the fused card is what it is given). Splitting a fused group needs pairwise scores among members and a clustering step; that is a design change to the ledger (D149 has no split operation) and stays an offline proposal.
    44	- **Identity (deduplication)**: a relevance reranker will rank every `related_not_same` pair near the top; without the key gate it would merge 2026 with 2027 and GAAP with non-GAAP. With the gate, the remaining true duplicates (CPU revenue under two names; the guarantee under three wordings) are decidable only from the initiating quotes, so arm (b) is the one to watch; expect at best "same-matter" from the text-only arm and `same` from the source-assisted arm on 2 of 3 gold sets, with the provisional C v18/v21 pair abstaining.
    45	- **Cost**: at the published rates the current matching call is already cheap (G week ≈ $0.18 at $0.10/$0.50); a local reranker saves money only if it replaces the call, and it cannot replace the dependency and evidence decisions the call also makes. The honest framing is quality and consistency, not cost.
    46	- **Stability**: a cross-encoder is deterministic; that alone removes the sample-to-sample membership variance we measured (0/2 then 2/2 for SK and NAVER across runs). This is the strongest argument for it in grouping.
    47	
