   299	thesis question + focus + key
   300	    against
   301	group name + scope + aliases + historically available member questions
   302	```
   303	
   304	Then run a separately labelled, source-assisted arm adding the relevant original passage. Do not claim that a thesis-text-only model recovered a relationship that was supplied in the passage.
   305	
   306	The source evidence is explicit:
   307	
   308	- SK’s press release says the factory will deploy Vera Rubin.
   309	- NAVER’s press release says the infrastructure will include Vera Rubin and Blackwell.
   310	- The NVIDIA transcript identifies Vera CPU as a Rubin-platform component.
   311	
   312	A source-assisted local reranker may recover all three without a generative call. That is a testable hypothesis. A model that answers from remembered product knowledge alone has not established a source-supported membership.
   313	
   314	Grouping must allow **zero, one or several memberships**. Nearest-centroid assignment to exactly one cluster would violate the contract. NVIDIA affiliation alone must not pull RTX Spark or an unrelated product into Rubin.
   315	
   316	For F1, use the nine G.g1 members as a small diagnostic:
   317	
   318	- Rollout/deployment candidates: v4, v5, v18, v19.
   319	- Aggregate investment candidates: v1, v12, v20.
   320	- Company capex cases needing an explicit membership judgment: v39, v42.
   321	- Missing candidate to evaluate: v13.
   322	
   323	Compare pairwise similarities and a hierarchical sub-clustering with an owner-reviewed division into matters. Do not require two clusters merely because two were requested in the hypothesis. Record whether the split is stable when one member is removed and whether a bridge thesis legitimately belongs to both matters.
   324	
   325	A reranker evaluated against the present broad G.g1 may reproduce F1 perfectly. That is why stored-membership agreement and desired membership quality need separate columns.
   326	
   327	**No split should be written by this POC.** D149 assigns association during extraction; the existing design has no settled post-creation group-split operation. Any later operation needs a decision about group identity, historical membership and future reads. An offline proposed split is useful evidence for that decision, not permission to perform it.
   328	
   329	For scalability, reuse member-thesis vectors. Embed the group description separately and retrieve a few relevant member examples for comparison. Re-embedding a growing concatenation of every member would make both cost and group meaning drift on every addition.
   330	
   331	**5. One evening can answer whether this direction is promising, not whether it is production-ready.**
   332	
   333	The smallest implementation is an offline evaluator with exact vector scans. At 45–94 theses per ledger, an approximate-nearest-neighbour service would add an unnecessary variable.
   334	
   335	| Stage | Inputs and work | Outputs | Estimated time and new API cost |
   336	|---|---|---|---|
   337	| A. Freeze and reconstruct | Completed snapshots; all 825 matching records; handovers, write records and archives | Manifest, decision table, original recall lists, exception classes | 30–45 min; $0 |
   338	| B. Fix the measuring stick | All 52 G candidate cards; 45 G thesis/group cards; 40 stratified evidence decisions; named C/S regressions; existing idea labels | Source-backed labels with required/optional/forbidden relations and unresolved cases | 60–120 min; $0 |
   339	| C. Offline controls | Original lists, exact keys, current aliases, cached idea embeddings | Baseline recall, identity and membership tables; positive controls | 30–45 min; $0 |
   340	| D. New recall | Text-only and key-enriched representations; G/E first; Chinese strata separately | Exact top-k results and per-case rank changes | 20–40 min; embedding estimate below |
   341	| E. Reranking | One local cross-encoder; optionally 24 paired generative comparisons | Error reasons, abstention coverage, paired cost and stability | 30–60 min locally; optional paid arm separately bounded |
   342	| F. Decision report | Frozen test configuration, all failures and unstarted cases | Six-layer acceptance report and a go/no-go recommendation | 30 min |
   343	
   344	The timing is an estimate. If label review consumes the evening, stop with a valid baseline. Do not replace missing labels with model agreement to finish on schedule.
   345	
   346	The 40 evidence cards should include 20 repeats, all four accepted G replacement decisions, and 16 new decisions, with hard negatives selected from the same story. Retellings of one story stay in one split.
   347	
   348	Use known Ohio/Rubin failures for development. Hold out entire other story families and later inputs for the frozen test. Parallel Chinese and English versions of the same source must stay together. These are holdouts for implementation choices, not an untouched external benchmark.
   349	
   350	The retrieval arms should be small:
   351	
   352	1. Current candidate lists.
   353	2. Exact-key candidates alone.
   354	3. Dense text recall.
   355	4. Dense key-enriched recall.
   356	5. A union of required key candidates, lexical candidates and dense candidates, ranked with a fixed fusion rule.
   357	
   358	Keep known exact identities outside a lossy top-k competition. Preserve broad retrieval for free scopes. Select one representation and fusion configuration on development data, then score the held-out set once.
   359	
   360	For local models, **BAAI/bge-m3** is a reasonable multilingual embedding comparison; its model card specifies 1,024-dimensional vectors and up to 8,192 tokens. **BAAI/bge-reranker-v2-m3** supplies a multilingual query–passage relevance score. Neither model card establishes financial identity accuracy. Pin the revision and inference settings, and measure local latency rather than borrowing benchmark claims. [BGE-M3 model card](https://huggingface.co/BAAI/bge-m3), [reranker model card](https://huggingface.co/BAAI/bge-reranker-v2-m3).
   361	
   362	For the first API embedding arm, use **`text-embedding-3-small`**, matching the existing index. Reserve `text-embedding-3-large` for a predeclared comparison if the smaller model’s specific failures justify it. Current published standard prices are **$0.02 and $0.13 per million input tokens** respectively. [Small model pricing](https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/models/text-embedding-3-small), [large model pricing](https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/models/text-embedding-3-large).
   363	
   364	The stored-text volume is small:
   365	
   366	| Representation population | Approximate tokens |
   367	|---|---:|
   368	| G: 45 theses + 52 candidates, key-enriched | 6,810 |
   369	| E: 60 theses + 75 candidates, key-enriched | 9,661 |
   370	| C: 40 theses + 61 candidates, key-enriched | 10,423 |
   371	| S: 94 theses + 137 candidates, key-enriched | 23,651 |
   372	| All four, one representation | 50,545 |
   373	| All stored and incoming evidence, with summaries, quotes and provenance | 537,896 |
   374	
   375	These are estimates from measured character volumes: ASCII characters divided by four, plus 1.5 tokens per non-ASCII character. They are not tokenizer measurements or billed usage. They deliberately precede cross-ledger text deduplication.
   376	
   377	Allowing **one million tokens** covers the proposed representations, evidence and reasonable overhead:
   378	
   379	- Small embeddings: about **$0.02**.
   380	- Large embeddings: about **$0.13**.
   381	- Proposed first-stage small-embedding cap: **$0.05**.
   382	
   383	Local inference has no API fee, but consumes setup time, memory and compute. It should not run alongside an active paid ingestion run.
   384	
   385	**The matching cost baseline needs two price columns.**
   386	
   387	Joining matching records to archives by request hash gives:
   388	
   389	| Snapshot | Unique archived matching requests | Input/output tokens | Archive cost estimate | Mean estimate/request | Median / p95 seconds |
   390	|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
   391	| G | 144 | 1,131,213 / 127,417 | $2.6882 | $0.01867 | 7.75 / 23.8 |
   392	| E | 208 | 1,595,147 / 187,554 | $3.8695 | $0.01860 | 7.3 / 21.7 |
   393	| C | 155 | 1,002,103 / 146,262 | $2.7152 | $0.01752 | 9.1 / 26.8 |
   394	
   395	These archive amounts use [model.py’s](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/model.py:26) fixed rates of $1.25/M input and $10/M output for `gpt-6-luna`; they are estimates, not invoices.
   396	
   397	The current official page instead lists **$0.10/M input and $0.50/M output**. At those standard rates, without cache discounts, G’s same token volume would be about **$0.1768 total**, or **$0.00123 per request**. Do not mix that repricing with the historical estimate and call the difference an algorithmic saving. [Current GPT-6 Luna pricing](https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/models/gpt-6-luna).
   398	
   399	An illustrative listwise reranker decision using **1,500 input and 200 total output tokens** costs:
   400	
   401	- **$0.003875** under the repository’s accounting rates.
   402	- **$0.00025** under the current published standard rates.
   403	
   404	Actual reasoning tokens must be included. Calling an LLM separately for ten pairs can erase the saving. Also, a current matching request often decides several evidence pairs and dependencies; a one-decision reranker is not a like-for-like replacement.
   405	
   406	If authorised, compare **24 inputs × three fresh samples × two arms = 144 requests**: unchanged current control versus the selected reranking intervention. Allocate 12 development and 12 held-out inputs, retaining every refusal. Expected cost under the conservative historical accounting is roughly $1.6; propose a **$3 total cap**, checked against the actual selected requests and output ceilings before launch. If that cap cannot cover the registered workload, return to Alex rather than shrink or raise it silently.
   407	
   408	Use separate archives per fresh replicate so stored-answer caching cannot masquerade as repeated sampling. Do not change the provider or model as part of the comparison.
   409	
   410	**The following are proposed acceptance numbers for Alex to approve, not adopted product gates.**
   411	
   412	| Layer from the governing acceptance contract | Proposed evening criterion |
   413	|---|---|
   414	| Content coverage | No loss of labelled important content in the tested path. Missing candidate creation remains visible and is not credited to retrieval. |
   415	| Membership meaning | All registered required relations recovered; zero forbidden relations. Report unreviewed and optional relations separately. |
   416	| Thesis identity | Zero false merges on explicit basis/unit/period/event negatives; recover every registered duplicate regression. Report automatic-decision coverage and abstentions. |
   417	| Evidence fidelity | Zero false repeat/replacement decisions on reviewed hard negatives; preserve every source reference. The disjoint-thesis SSI case is reported separately if the allowed search boundary cannot reach it. |
   418	| Sequential behaviour | Zero future-target leakage; exact reconstruction of tested candidate and membership visibility; missing prerequisites fail the evaluator. |
   419	| Stability and cost | Recall@10 at least 95%, Recall@20 at least 99% on reviewed eligible targets, with exact counts; no loss of a critical target versus control. Three fresh model samples must satisfy critical cases individually. Proposed warm retrieval p95 ≤100 ms and local top-20 reranking p95 ≤2 s on the actual machine. |
   420	
   421	For small positive denominators, the recall percentages effectively require every target. If a stratum has no positives, report **unexercised**. Do not average a wrong merge away with many easy “new” decisions.
   422	
   423	A stringent combined gate can fail a noisy control by chance. For illustration, 36 independent decisions each correct with probability 0.95 all pass only about 16% of the time. Measure fresh control against fresh control before interpreting a combined pass/fail. Repeated calls and related articles are correlated, so they do not create 36 independent business cases.
   424	
   425	A negative result is still useful:
   426	
   427	- Dense recall fails to recover source-reviewed targets already found by the baseline.
   428	- A reranker ranks related but wrong quantities above the correct one.
   429	- Improvements depend on manually inserting “Vera” or other test-specific aliases.
   430	- Grouping only reproduces the broad G.g1.
   431	- The system achieves zero errors only by abstaining on nearly everything.
   432	- Savings disappear once all matching responsibilities and fallbacks are included.
   433	- Gold disagreements remain unresolved.
   434	
   435	Any failed registered acceptance stops that line. Read and classify the reasons before proposing another configuration, as required by [AGENTS §§6–7](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/AGENTS.md).
   436	
   437	**The evaluator needs a small contract before implementation.**
   438	
   439	| State/event | Required behaviour |
   440	|---|---|
   441	| Input database is still receiving writes | Use the frozen sequence cutoff; verify selected-row digests before and after |
   442	| Missing archive, source or write mapping | Mark incomplete and exclude from success claims; do not infer an answer |
   443	| No eligible earlier destination | Label a new-item case, not a recall success |
   444	| Embedding or reranking fails | Retain the failure and denominator; do not silently substitute another method |
   445	| Configuration or representation changes | Create a new named result set; never overwrite the control |
   446	| Run is interrupted and resumed | Resume pending work by manifest/query identity; preserve completed results |
   447	| Test stage completes or fails | Seal its configuration and result; no automatic next arm |
   448	
   449	The manifest feeds every stage. Query representations feed only retrieval. Gold labels feed only scoring, never query construction. Rankings feed the comparator. Proposed decisions feed an offline comparison report, never the product ledger.
   450	
   451	Before paid work, run the required stored-answer preflight. Before any future integration, preserve existing source, allowed-target, identity, succession, dependency-cycle, replacement-date and historical-read checks. A changed shortlist changes the request; a cached old answer can test the evaluator but cannot establish the new model behaviour.
   452	
   453	**6. The principal limits concern information and authority, not vector storage.**
   454	
   455	- **Historical leakage:** filter by arrival sequence, not the model’s calendar knowledge or the final database. Member examples, evidence and aliases must also be historically available. Model knowledge may suggest a relationship, but only supplied contemporary sources can justify it.
   456	- **Small and dependent samples:** one week, one dominant company and repeated outlets do not establish market-wide reliability. Report results by story, task, language and key provenance. Zero errors in 30 independent cases still permits roughly a 10% error rate under the usual “rule of three” approximation.
   457	- **Mixed languages:** test English→English, Chinese→Chinese and cross-language retrieval separately. Never use a shared local `v1` as identity across ledgers. Do not translate stored source text or treat cross-language similarity as identity proof.
   458	- **Re-embedding cost:** immutable thesis text needs embedding once per model and representation version. Evidence arrivals should not re-embed its thesis. The existing hash-based design already supports this. Group descriptions and changing member context need separate versioning.
   459	- **Lost distinctions:** averaging vectors can blur period, scope and accounting basis. Exact fields remain outside the similarity score.
   460	- **Remaining LLM work:** deciding which uncovered matters warrant theses; understanding unresolved scope differences; assessing materiality; interpreting ambiguous revisions and source authority; choosing dependencies; and judging evidence against a ladder remain semantic work until separately validated.
   461	- **Retrieval cannot supply missing facts:** if extraction omitted financing content or stored an incorrect period, a better rank cannot make the record correct.
   462	
   463	**Only the following choices need Alex’s decision.** File placement, report fields and evaluator organisation are implementation choices.
   464	
   465	| Choice | Options and recommendation |
   466	|---|---|
   467	| What constitutes the semantic gold? | Approve or revise the CPU and financing duplicate labels, spending-scope distinctions and idea #5. **Recommend resolving these before scoring a new arm.** |
   468	| What may the first POC change? | Shadow retrieval only, or automatic decisions. **Recommend shadow retrieval and offline decision proposals; keep the current pipeline authoritative.** |
   469	| May evidence recall cross thesis boundaries? | Preserve within-thesis search, or test a narrow provenance-aware lookup for already stored statements. **Recommend testing the narrow lookup offline against SSI, without authorising broad semantic merging.** |
   470	| What should happen to a group containing two matters? | Leave it as stored, or design a historically versioned split operation. **Recommend an offline proposed split first; no ledger mutation.** |
   471	| Which next-stage budget is authorised? | $0 controls only; add small embeddings capped at $0.05; optionally add the paired reranker arm capped at $3. **Recommend embeddings after label approval, with generative reranking conditional on the measured remaining error class.** |
