    64	- **G.e133/e134:** the same July 27 NVIDIA statement about SSI increasing compute through Vera Rubin access. This is a missed consolidation.
    65	- **G.e17/e47:** identical inventory/capacity language attributed to February and May company statements. Identical wording across different statement dates does not establish one evidence identity.
    66	
    67	G.e133/e134 exposes a search-boundary problem. The first copy links to G.v5, the shipment thesis. The second links to G.v4, the product-revenue thesis. At sequence 87, match record 50 searches only G.v4’s evidence, so G.e133 is unavailable. The model reasonably reports no matching supplied evidence.
    68	
    69	A better embedding within the same search boundary cannot recover that copy. The narrow structural question is whether an exact, provenance-aware lookup may find an already stored statement across thesis boundaries before normal within-thesis comparison. Broad global semantic evidence merging would be a much larger change.
    70	
