    55	
    56	## 4. Implementation on this repository's data (offline, nothing written to any ledger)
    57	
    58	1. **Snapshot and reconstruct** (Codex's stage A): for every stored matching decision (G 145, E 210, C 155, S 315 records), rebuild the library as it stood at that sequence number (open theses, keys, groups with members), the candidate as the handover carried it, and the destination actually written (`processing_steps` step `written`, since `match_records.written_json` is null everywhere).
    59	2. **Recall text and cards**: thesis card = object name, question, focus, key fields written out; group card = name, scope, aliases, up to five member focuses. Version both strings; store the hash.
    60	3. **Recall arms** (exact scans, no index server at this size): current lists; exact-key candidates; dense top-20 (`text-embedding-3-small`, the index the idea route already uses; ≈ 50k tokens for all four ledgers, about $0.001; local `bge-m3` as the free twin); union with a fixed fusion. Metric: recall@k against the gold `same` targets and, for groups, against required members' groups.
    61	4. **Rerank arms**: (a) Jev, state = candidate card only; (b) Jev, state = candidate card plus its initiating quote (source-assisted; S v16/v48 and the NAVER and Vera CPU memberships are only decidable from the quote); (c) `bge-reranker-v2-m3` on the same pairs, the free fallback while the Jev account is unpaid and the second opinion once it is; (d) gpt-6-luna listwise over the top 10 with the same labels, 3 fresh samples, as the control that is also the current method. Jev arms: about 52 G candidates × 2 arms ≈ 104 calls, 1–3k tokens each, under $0.02 (plus a first probe of a few hundred tokens once the account is paid); (c) $0 local; (d) ≈ 24 inputs × 3 samples × 2 arms ≈ 144 calls ≈ $0.04 at the published rates. Jev is deterministic in the sense that matters (one call, one distribution); re-send 10 states twice to measure whether its probabilities move.
    62	5. **Decision layer** (code, measured on the dev half of the gold, scored once on the rest): key gate first (different basis, unit, period, event type, or code-owned scope → not the same; same complete code-owned key → the same, as today); then the reranker score with two thresholds: above the high one → `same` (identity) only if the key allows; between → `same-matter` (membership); below → `different`; an `abstain` band where the generative call is still asked. For groups: a thesis joins every group whose card scores above the membership threshold, zero or several, never exactly one.
    63	6. **Report** by the six layers of the grouping-acceptance contract: content coverage (a reranker cannot create the missing OpenAI thesis), membership (required recovered / forbidden produced), identity (false merges on the hard negatives, duplicates recovered), evidence fidelity (not in this POC), sequential behaviour (no future thesis in any list), stability and cost (three samples, per-decision cost at the published rates).
    64	
    65	Budget: $0 for steps 1–2 and the local arms; ≤ $0.05 embeddings; ≤ $0.10 for the generative control at the published rates. One evening if the gold holds; stop with the baseline if the labels need work.
    66	
    67	## 5. The outcome to expect, said before running
    68	
    69	- **Recall**: already saturated on a library of 45–94 theses; dense recall will match or slightly beat the trigram lists (the idea route's cached vectors already put the gold thesis in the top 10 for 17–18 of 18 ideas, Codex §2). No visible product change from recall alone.
    70	- **Grouping**: this is where a reranker should pay, but only with the source in hand. Checked on the stored g1 card (name, scope, aliases `Blackwell`, `Rubin`, `AI factories`, `AI infrastructure`) against the thesis texts: Meta's and Microsoft's capex theses share `capital`, `infrastructure`, `investment` with the card, so a text-only cross-encoder should recover them (the F7 misses) and should reject RTX Spark and NemoClaw, which share nothing; the hard negative is NVIDIA's consolidated revenue (shares `infrastructure`, `revenue`), which the threshold must keep out. The Vera CPU thesis shares only `revenue` with the card and the NAVER factory shares nothing: both are joinable only through the source text (the press release says the factory runs Vera Rubin and Blackwell; the transcript says Vera is the Rubin platform's CPU), which is how the model joined NAVER in poc-v21 (it read the source while citing the group). So arm (b), the reranker given the candidate's initiating quote, is the one that can recover Vera CPU and NAVER; arm (a) cannot, and a hit from it would mean the cross-encoder answered from remembered product knowledge, which does not count. Expect it to fail on F1 (it will rank the capex aggregates and the Rubin shipments both highly against the fused g1, because the fused card is what it is given). Splitting a fused group needs pairwise scores among members and a clustering step; that is a design change to the ledger (D149 has no split operation) and stays an offline proposal.
    71	- **Jev specifically** (an estimate from the old repository's measurements, not a measurement here): on membership with the quote in the state, the shape of its 81.2%-alone / 88.4%-in-cascade evidence-relation result, so expect it to recover the Rubin and capex required members and reject the forbidden ones with the threshold set on dev, and to leave a few `cannot_tell` for the matching call; on identity, the shape of its 74% role result: usable as a flag that disagrees with the model, not as the decider. If Jev's probabilities turn out well calibrated on the dev half (a `belongs` at 0.9 is right nine times in ten), the cascade is the design; if they are not, Jev is only a recall-side filter.
    72	- **Identity (deduplication)**: a relevance reranker will rank every `related_not_same` pair near the top; without the key gate it would merge 2026 with 2027 and GAAP with non-GAAP. With the gate, the remaining true duplicates (CPU revenue under two names; the guarantee under three wordings) are decidable only from the initiating quotes, so arm (b) is the one to watch; expect at best "same-matter" from the text-only arm and `same` from the source-assisted arm on 2 of 3 gold sets, with the provisional C v18/v21 pair abstaining.
    73	- **Cost**: at the published rates the current matching call is already cheap (G week ≈ $0.18 at $0.10/$0.50); a local reranker saves money only if it replaces the call, and it cannot replace the dependency and evidence decisions the call also makes. The honest framing is quality and consistency, not cost.
    74	- **Stability**: a cross-encoder is deterministic; that alone removes the sample-to-sample membership variance we measured (0/2 then 2/2 for SK and NAVER across runs). This is the strongest argument for it in grouping.
